WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.040
The Link spacecraft re-entered over the South Pacific after failing its rescue mission for the Swift Observatory. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 205. The Link spacecraft came down at 1438 UTC on September 24. The craft had been meant to help the Swift Observatory, but could not complete that task. Orbital analyst Jonathan McDowell recorded the re-entry details. No efforts to recover any debris were reported after the event.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.180
On a different note, the Coast Bar Satellite designation scheme has followed a specific

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.960
rule since 1963.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.460
Letters I and O are left out to prevent mix-ups with the numbers 1 and 0.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.900
The letter Q stays in the system without any problem.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.340
This approach has stayed in place for more than 60 years of satellite tracking.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.160
The rule keeps identification records consistent around the world.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.080
Engineers are now getting ready for the next Starship Test Flight.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.540
The goal is to reach orbital velocity for the first time.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.560
Earlier flights included suborbital hops and heat shield checks.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.340
Success here would count as a key step in the overall program.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.240
The flight plan centers on proving full orbital insertion.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.060
More nations are looking at joining the Russia-China lunar project.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.060
The effort focuses on building a shared research station on the moon.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.420
Talks continue with space agencies that have shown interest.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.600
interest. The plan calls for bringing in partners beyond the original two countries. Several

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.800
nations with newer space programs are part of the conversations. Pentagon contracts have

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.980
gone mostly to SpaceX for launch work lately. Other firms have raised worries about fewer

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:44.280
chances for their own bids. The deals involve moving national security payloads into orbit.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.600
Several recent awards centered on SpaceX for steady launch access.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.460
industry watchers point to the heavy focus on one company.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.500
Tulane University in New Orleans is adding space research to its engineering work.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.400
Faculty members are starting projects on satellite technology and propulsion systems.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The move fits with rising local interest in aerospace fields.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.320
New classes and research centers are in the planning stages.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.920
Local industry ties are being explored to support the growth.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.640
NASA's balloon program has already seen four successful flights this fall

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.980
from Fort Sumner, New Mexico. Two more missions are set for the weeks ahead.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.940
Hurricane Polo is changing upper-level winds and affecting wind launches can happen.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.760
The team keeps watching weather patterns to keep operations safe.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.120
Earlier flights carried science instruments high into the atmosphere for study.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.820
Engineers at Marshall Space Flight Center finished tests on a new CubeSat.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.640
The craft uses a dual-mode propulsion setup to cut down on volatile fuels.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:53.360
The layout is meant to make space maneuvers simpler overall, checks covered vibration, heat and vacuum conditions to confirm how it performs.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.320
The CubeSat is now ready for final assembly before its launch.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:04.140
Hubble took a picture of spiral galaxy NGC 4698 that shows hidden motion inside.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.260
The arms look calm from outside, but hold signs of disorder within.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.060
The image came out on September 18th, 2026.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.520
It reveals features that do not match the galaxy's quiet outer look.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.100
Telescope data point to areas of unexpected star activity in the disk.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.020
A new set of images shows the photon sphere around a black hole.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.380
Light there can loop enclosed paths without end.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.620
The boundary appears as the line separating the black hole from the sky.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.440
Gravity pulls starlight from Orion into repeated views.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.920
The path of each photon curves back on itself at that exact distance.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.260
Another sequence shows the approach to the photon sphere.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.400
Light bends more sharply the closer the view gets.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.620
Starlight gains energy and shifts toward blue as it nears the edge.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.960
Multiple copies of background stars show up in the frame.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.020
The distortion grows stronger with each step inward.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.180
A third view starts inside the photon sphere.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.960
The opening frame is dark because it looks straight into the black hole.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.480
Stars return when the gaze turns upward, now heavily blue-shifted.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.440
Faint stars stand out because gravity has changed their light paths.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.700
The change from dark to bright distorted fields happens quickly in the sequence.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.380
A technical bulletin warns about aluminum alloy 2219 used in aerospace parts.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.200
Bad casting or forging can leave tiny defects in the metal.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.240
Those defects raise the chance of corrosion after surface treatments.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.280
The note explains the risks for hardware that flies in space.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:43.480
case. Careful production steps are needed to keep the material sound. A 1,998 program at

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.940
the Smithsonian looked at whether cosmology was finished. Peoples and Turner joined other

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.140
researchers for the discussion. NASA and the National Science Foundation helped sponsor

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:59.520
the event. The talks reviewed the state of the field at that moment. The full program

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.640
is still kept as a record of the debate. NASA put out a free mini-poster based on web telescope

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.220
data of Centaurus A. The image brings out the galaxy's dust lanes and active center.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.880
The file sits in the science.nasa.gov asset library for anyone to download.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.260
People can use it for teaching or personal projects.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.780
The poster goes along with the latest pictures of the galaxy.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.840
The photon sphere images make clear what happens when gravity traps light in loops.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.000
Background stars show up several times because rays circled the black hole first.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.580
light shifts blue as it climbs out of the gravity well, yet gains energy from the bend.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.200
The spheres hits at the exact spot where light speed matches orbital speed.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.100
One question left open is how much more detailed polarized light could reveal in similar views.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.100
If a black hole held the mass of the sun inside a space the size of a city block,

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.100
the photon sphere would sit at about three times that blocks width.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.740
A photon traveling there would see the same sky section repeat every few microseconds as

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.040
its path bends back.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:11.360
Temperatures reach millions of degrees from squeezed material, yet the light stays in

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:11.680
orbit.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.000
Radio measurements have pinned the size of the region around our galaxy's black hole to

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.040
within a few percent.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:24.080
Event horizon telescope pictures already capture the effect, but the exact role of nearby

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.540
magnetic fields in twisting those paths remains unknown.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:32.240
Keep an eye on the next Starship Test Flight for signs of orbital progress.

